&lt;p&gt;As a Quality Engineer at Neros, you’ll own electro-mechanical build quality across the factory as we scale production, from motors, harnesses, and mechanisms through the finished vehicle. You’ll be the first call when an assembly or integration issue shows up on the line, working with manufacturing and engineering to contain it, find the root cause, and make sure every unit survives and performs in contested environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We’re looking for a hands-on engineer with electro-mechanical hardware manufacturing experience, a proactive, ownership-driven approach to solving problems, and a proven ability to perform in fast-moving production environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Responsibilities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own day-to-day electro-mechanical build quality across the factory as the go-to engineer for any assembly and integration issue that surfaces, from components and sub-assemblies such as motors, harnesses, and mechanisms through final vehicle assembly&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Act as first responder for quality issues on the line: disposition defects, contain suspect product, and run MRB for electro-mechanical nonconformances&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lead root cause investigations on electro-mechanical defects in partnership with engineering, and verify that fixes hold on the line&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Track line metrics (FPY, defect Pareto, scrap and rework, escapes) and use SPC to catch drift before it becomes a failure&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run regular build quality reviews on defect trends and drive the top issues to closure&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Partner with manufacturing and engineering to add in-line checks and error-proofing where the data calls for it, reducing inspection as processes prove capable&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drive design for quality (DFQ) by feeding line defect data and inspection findings back to engineering to improve manufacturability and reduce defects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create and maintain inspection plans and first article inspections for new builds, design revisions, and process changes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review work instructions and inspection criteria to keep requirements clear, objective, and verifiable&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Certify inspectors and operators to workmanship standards, and own and audit the measurement and calibration program, including gauges, torque tools, and Gage R&amp;amp;R, across the factory&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
